SOFTFP(3)                      P32W Unix                       SOFTFP(3)

NAME
       softfp, fp_add, fp_sub, fp_mul, fp_div, fp_sqrt - floating point

SYNOPSIS
       #include <softfp.h>

       typedef struct { unsigned lo, hi; } fp64;

       void fp_add(fp64 *r, const fp64 *a, const fp64 *b);
       (also fp_sub, fp_mul, fp_div, fp_pow)
       void fp_sqrt(fp64 *r, const fp64 *a);
       (also fp_neg, fp_abs, fp_trunc, fp_floor, fp_round, fp_exp,
       fp_log, fp_sin, fp_cos, fp_tan, fp_atan)
       void fp_ldexp(fp64 *r, const fp64 *a, int n);
       int fp_cmp(const fp64 *a, const fp64 *b);
       int fp_isnan(const fp64 *a), fp_isinf(...), fp_iszero(...),
           fp_sign(...), fp_isint(...), fp_ilogb(...);
       void fp_fromint(fp64 *r, int i);
       void fp_fromuint(fp64 *r, unsigned u);
       int fp_toint(const fp64 *a, int *ok);
       const char *fp_parse(fp64 *r, const char *s);
       int fp_digits(const fp64 *a, int ndig, char *digits, int *exp10);
       char *fp_format(char *buf, const fp64 *a, int ndig);

DESCRIPTION
       P32W has no floating-point hardware, so floating point is this
       library (cc's double uses it underneath, and math(3) is built
       on it; programs normally just use double): IEEE 754 double
       precision (53-bit significand, about 15-16 digits, exponents
       to 10^308, with subnormals, infinities and NaNs) done with
       integer instructions. An fp64 holds the 64 bits of a double.

       Every function takes pointers, the result first, since cc
       cannot pass or return structures: fp_mul(&r, &a, &b) is r =
       a * b. The result may be an operand.

       fp_add, fp_sub, fp_mul, fp_div and fp_sqrt are correctly
       rounded (to nearest, ties to even): the same bits as any IEEE
       machine. fp_exp, fp_log, fp_sin, fp_cos, fp_tan and fp_atan
       work in 62 bits and round once: correctly rounded for 99.5% or
       more of arguments, otherwise the double next to it (math(3)).
       fp_pow works y log x in 96 bits, and is correctly rounded
       too. Out of their domain (sqrt or log of a negative number)
       they give a NaN.

       fp_cmp returns -1, 0 or 1, or 2 if either is a NaN. fp_toint
       truncates; *ok becomes 0 (and the result saturates) out of the
       int range. fp_round rounds halves away from zero.

       fp_parse reads [sign] digits [. digits] [e [sign] digits] and
       returns where it stopped, or 0 if there was no number.
       fp_digits gives ndig (1-17) significant digits, rounded, and
       the power of ten of the first. fp_format writes printf %g style
       ("1.5", "1e+20", "inf", "nan"); 17 digits read back exactly.

EXAMPLES
              fp64 a, b, r;
              char buf[32];
              fp_parse(&a, "2");
              fp_sqrt(&r, &a);
              printf("%s\n", fp_format(buf, &r, 15));
                                      /* prints 1.4142135623731 */

SEE ALSO
       basic(1)

BUGS
       Each operation is a function call of a few hundred
       instructions: thousands of operations a second at 1 MIPS.
